00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:18.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah?

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.970
That sounds like
it'd be really cool.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:20.890
Awesome.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:21.848
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:02:21.848 --> 00:02:23.776
So let's go talk with
our colleague Jen Tong.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:24.650
MARK MANDEL: Why not?

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:25.340
Let's do it.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.970
So we're joined today by one of
our most illustrious coworkers,

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.255
Jen Tong.

00:02:31.255 --> 00:02:32.494
Jen, how are you doing today?

00:02:32.494 --> 00:02:33.410
JEN TONG: Doing great.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:33.940
How are you?

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:34.830
MARK MANDEL: Very, very well.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.455
Thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:02:36.455 --> 00:02:37.570
JEN TONG: Of course.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:38.945
MARK MANDEL: So
we wanted to talk

00:02:38.945 --> 00:02:42.360
to you about Internet of Things
and that sort of fun stuff.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.840
But why don't you
let us know who

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.710
you are, what you do,
your background, so people

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:47.995
have an idea of who you are.

00:02:47.995 --> 00:02:48.870
JEN TONG: Sure thing.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:49.890
So hi, everybody.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:50.630
I'm Jen.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.780
I'm a developer
advocate at Google.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.820
And I focus on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.730
That means, in a nutshell, that
I help people cause trouble

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.740
with code or build cool
stuff and then, you

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.930
know, join them in the
satisfaction of accomplishing

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.180
something great.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.410
And along the way, I
help people understand

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.370
why cloud technologies
are awesome and can

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:11.944
make their life easier.

00:03:11.944 --> 00:03:12.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:13.837
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:03:13.837 --> 00:03:15.920
Do you focus on any
particular products and stuff?

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.620
I mean, obviously, we want to
talk about Internet of Things.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.037
But I'm just kind of curious.

00:03:19.037 --> 00:03:19.620
JEN TONG: Yep.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.390
So kind of the
area I focus on is

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.724
parts of the Cloud to either
make IoT prototyping really

00:03:24.724 --> 00:03:27.140
easy-- which is I guess what
we'll be talking a little bit

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.900
more about today-- but also
the other side of the equation,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.580
is Internet of Things devices
are really good at generating

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.839
a whole bunch of data.

00:03:33.839 --> 00:03:35.880
So I spend a lot of time
focusing on the big data

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.890
side of that, things
like BigQuery and Pub/Sub

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:39.535
and Bigtable.

00:03:39.535 --> 00:03:40.451
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:03:40.451 --> 00:03:41.261
Yes.

00:03:41.261 --> 00:03:41.760
Cool.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.130
So I'm a little bit curious
about the Internet of Things.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.930
I've heard many
definitions of it.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.174
What is yours?

00:03:48.174 --> 00:03:49.840
JEN TONG: The Internet
of Things the way

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.016
I define it is any device that
didn't used to be associated

00:03:55.016 --> 00:03:56.640
with internet
connection-- so something

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.280
smaller than a laptop,
smaller than a smartphone,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.170
usually-- that connects
to the internet

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.410
and then allows you to access
it in ways that you did not

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.860
previously expect, so
using the same [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.330
protocols and other
languages that we use to do

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.080
anything else on the internet.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.247
MARK MANDEL: So what would
be some examples of that?

00:04:14.247 --> 00:04:15.913
JEN TONG: So one of
my favorite examples

00:04:15.913 --> 00:04:18.260
is something that people
don't really usually associate

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:22.720
with the Internet of Things,
is the rise of Uber and Lyft

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.559
and the other internet-connected
ride share services.

00:04:26.559 --> 00:04:28.100
And this is because
it took something

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.391
that didn't use to be connected
to the internet-- which

00:04:30.391 --> 00:04:33.820
was essentially like cars, ride
share, taxi cabs-- and then

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.980
connected it to the internet
using people's smartphones,

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.080
which was really cool.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.240
And then suddenly it
became a lot easier to use,

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.150
and it spawned a whole
new marketplace, which

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.994
was kind of an amazing thing.

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:46.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:50.100
I never thought about Uber or
Lyft being Internet of Things,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.290
but it totally makes sense--
the internet of cars.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:52.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:53.338
It's really cool.

00:04:53.338 --> 00:04:54.150
OK, interesting.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.180
You could almost-- OK, so would
you classify, like, Airbnb

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.660
as an Internet of Things?

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:00.651
JEN TONG: Airbnb?

00:05:00.651 --> 00:05:02.650
I don't know as much,
because there's not really

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.817
a lot of, like-- there's no
specific hardware involved.

00:05:04.817 --> 00:05:05.244
MARK MANDEL: True.

00:05:05.244 --> 00:05:07.000
JEN TONG: Airbnb
more, to me, seems

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.610
like a hub of payment gateways.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:09.260
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:09.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's more-- OK.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:10.190
Fair enough.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:11.690
JEN TONG: Which is
pretty cool, too,

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.440
it's just-- the houses
don't move as much.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.327
They don't need that.

00:05:15.327 --> 00:05:16.410
MARK MANDEL: That is true.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.460
JEN TONG: I mean, the
moment they add, like,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.670
location specific
temperature predictions

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.230
and stuff like that, then
that would probably put them

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.900
over the edge, which
would be pretty cool.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:24.090
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:24.460
JEN TONG: Little
weather stations--

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:24.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Like Nest.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:25.500
JEN TONG: --at each house.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:25.825
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:26.940
Well it's like Nest, really.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:27.565
JEN TONG: Yeah.

00:05:27.565 --> 00:05:28.515
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:28.515 --> 00:05:29.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Actually, when I

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.265
thought about Internet of
Things, one of the first things

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:35.770
that came to mind
was a toothbrush.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.630
Can you talk a little
bit more about that,

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:39.120
like it's also
Internet of Things

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.665
when you start connecting random
stuff like toothbrush or Nest--

00:05:43.665 --> 00:05:46.335
like thermostats-- is also
that Internet of Things?

00:05:46.335 --> 00:05:47.710
JEN TONG: Yeah,
we're seeing that

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:49.250
happen all over the place now.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.670
Things that used to just be
standalone, and had their own--

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:53.870
potentially their own APIs.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.203
They might've had a way to
digitally interface with them

00:05:56.203 --> 00:05:57.467
over Bluetooth or something.

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:59.050
But suddenly, they're
starting to come

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.750
into existence with web servers
or other proprietary ways

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.380
of pushing data to the
Cloud like toothbrushes,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.830
refrigerators, washing machines.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.349
And it's suddenly making
them a lot more functional.

00:06:09.349 --> 00:06:10.890
And then, magically,
you can use them

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.284
from any internet connected
device, which is cool.

00:06:13.284 --> 00:06:14.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.400
MARK MANDEL: That sounds good.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.930
And soon, you know,
my fridge can tell me

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.055
about all the stuff I really
should have thrown out

00:06:19.055 --> 00:06:20.264
but haven't yet already.

00:06:20.264 --> 00:06:21.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.370
I'm still waiting for the
day when the fridge will

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.360
be able to send a
notification to my phone,

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:26.600
saying as like, hey.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.450
You're running out of
milk-- stuff like that

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:29.410
MARK MANDEL: I want
my fridge to send

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:30.680
a note to Amazon saying, hey.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.020
Just send him some
milk automatically

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:32.880
because he's already run out.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.520
And then I don't even
have to do anything.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:35.936
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's real lazy.

00:06:35.936 --> 00:06:36.870
I like it.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.080
Nice.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.300
So, tell us a little bit
about how did you get involved

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.814
with the Internet of Things?

00:06:42.814 --> 00:06:45.230
JEN TONG: So I'm going to have
to go further back on that.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:48.300
My education
university was focused

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.345
on both electrical and
computer engineering.

00:06:51.345 --> 00:06:52.720
So at the beginning
of my career,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.130
I spent time working on
things like robotics.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:58.200
In fact, I had a little
run doing robotics at NASA.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.800
And I actually
hated it back then.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:02.467
It was really hard
because we didn't

00:07:02.467 --> 00:07:04.550
have all the cool debugging
and all the neat stuff

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:08.530
that the internet was bringing
to other kinds of development.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.700
So I took a break and
focused on web development

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:14.280
for most of my career because
debuggers are awesome.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.080
And then I kind
of pushed it aside

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.669
until one day,
someone's like hey.

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:19.960
There's this new Arduino thing.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.000
Electronics is easy now.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.830
And I didn't
believe it at first,

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.999
but I picked up an Arduino
starter kid and I was hooked.

00:07:26.999 --> 00:07:28.540
And then it was just
a matter of time

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.910
before Wi-Fi shields
and other things

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:32.820
and allowed those
easy-to-use electronics

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.120
to start getting connected
to the internet appeared

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:36.130
on the market.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.830
And then suddenly, it
was fun and easy to build

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.953
stuff that used to be
kind of a pain to build.

00:07:40.953 --> 00:07:41.869
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:07:41.869 --> 00:07:45.010
I'm a little bit curious
about the robot part.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.680
What kinds of robots
were you building?

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:48.610
Can you share that?

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.430
Because it was NASA, so I'm
not sure if you could say,

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:51.221
if you could share.

00:07:51.221 --> 00:07:53.572
JEN TONG: Oh, they were
all public projects that

00:07:53.572 --> 00:07:54.780
have all been canceled since.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.990
But one of the ones I
spend the most time on

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.740
was called the crew
return vehicle,

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:01.900
which was the escape pod
for the space station.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.362
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:03.362 --> 00:08:04.820
JEN TONG: It's
since been canceled,

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:07.480
but the idea behind it was it
was a mostly automated return

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:09.650
system, so you
wouldn't have to have

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:12.440
as much training for the
pilots on the space station.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:15.260
And I worked on a
physical robot test bed

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.920
for the software that did
things like automated docking

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:19.487
and release.

00:08:19.487 --> 00:08:20.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's awesome.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:21.778
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:08:21.778 --> 00:08:25.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But you're
a Cloud developer advocate.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:29.254
So how does the
Cloud get in touch

00:08:29.254 --> 00:08:30.420
with the Internet of Things?

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:31.780
Are they the same thing?

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:32.878
How are they related?

00:08:32.878 --> 00:08:34.669
JEN TONG: So it's kind
of the internet part

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:36.918
of the Internet of Things,
because all the electronics

00:08:36.918 --> 00:08:38.380
and the embedded
operating systems

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.230
and all that stuff makes a thing
that could be very intelligent.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.050
But once you attach
it to the internet,

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:48.885
Cloud is a logical way to
add internet capabilities.

00:08:48.885 --> 00:08:51.010
MARK MANDEL: So are there
any particular challenges

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:51.790
on the Cloud side?

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:53.850
Like I'm guessing if you have
lots of Internet of Things,

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.190
you need to deal
with like scale,

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:56.685
or you talked a little
bit about big data.

00:08:56.685 --> 00:08:58.445
Could you tell us a little
more about how that works?

00:08:58.445 --> 00:08:59.028
JEN TONG: Yes.

00:08:59.028 --> 00:09:00.712
There's a couple
that come to mind.

00:09:00.712 --> 00:09:02.170
The first one is
Internet of Things

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.460
devices tend not to be the
most intelligent systems

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.918
because you're going to want
to use lower power things that

00:09:06.918 --> 00:09:09.975
are less smart, so they tend to
generate a lot of data, which

00:09:09.975 --> 00:09:12.350
is cool, because you can do
cool things with lots of data

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.000
like machine learning
and all that stuff,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.570
but it is a different kind
of data than what we're

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.230
used to from web development.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.410
So it stresses a lot of our
traditional database and REST

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.736
API systems that we've
built up to power web apps.

00:09:25.736 --> 00:09:27.360
So we have to kind
of take a little bit

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.320
of a different approach to
handling that much throughput

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:31.750
of data, which is where things
like Pub/Sub and Bigtable

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:33.140
really shine.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:36.470
Another aspect of it is
Internet of Things is also

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.310
about low power systems.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:42.449
And transport layer security
is usually a big challenge,

00:09:42.449 --> 00:09:43.990
and actually is
responsible for a lot

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.310
of the complexity in
sending data in a secure way

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:47.410
to the internet.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:50.470
So that's one that you
usually spend a lot of watts

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:53.160
and a lot of effort overcoming.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.742
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do you have
some advice about security

00:09:55.742 --> 00:09:57.450
on the Internet of
Things, because that's

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:59.640
one of the things that,
every single time I read

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.910
about Internet of
Things on the newspaper,

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:04.980
is because someone
hacked-- like, I read

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.140
about someone
hacking a toothbrush

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.580
and they were able to
access your password

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:10.680
or something like that.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.600
Like what kind of techniques
you should implement

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:15.884
to avoid those things?

00:10:15.884 --> 00:10:17.300
JEN TONG: What
kind of techniques?

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.910
A lot of this goes back to
kind of the same techniques

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:22.380
we're used to doing from
other kinds of development.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.171
The first one that I
would always recommend

00:10:24.171 --> 00:10:26.210
is don't reinvent
security yourself.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.250
We have protocols like
transport layer security

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:30.870
that we are pretty
confident work really well.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:31.650
Use those.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.608
Don't try and invent your
own encryption scheme

00:10:33.608 --> 00:10:36.030
to save a few bits of overhead.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:39.950
The other aspect is--
hard off is a good way

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.230
to make sure that things
like microphones and things

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.870
can stay off when
you're not actually

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:46.500
using them for your device.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:47.416
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:10:47.416 --> 00:10:49.592
JEN TONG: So take
advantage of physical off.

00:10:49.592 --> 00:10:51.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Unplug
it and that's it.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:51.680
Nice.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:52.200
JEN TONG: Exactly.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:53.150
MARK MANDEL: The
most secure computer

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.070
is one that's not plugged
to the internet or power.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:55.986
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:55.986 --> 00:10:57.560
That's-- that
normally tends to--

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:58.230
MARK MANDEL: That works.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:10:59.771
Well, that sounds
really interesting.

00:10:59.771 --> 00:11:01.360
So if people want
to get involved

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.360
with the Internet of Things
or they're sort of interested

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.318
in that sort of platform,
where should they go?

00:11:05.318 --> 00:11:07.016
What should they do?

00:11:07.016 --> 00:11:08.390
JEN TONG: So most
of the people I

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:09.890
talk to who are
just kind of getting

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:11.850
into the Internet
of Things excitement

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:13.810
have web development background.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.530
So this is where I kind of steer
them in a specific direction,

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.320
is there's this really cool
growing community of people

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:20.880
building NodeBots.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.770
And NodeBots is mostly
focused on the electronic side

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:27.260
and using JavaScript to
control robots and electronics.

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.860
But because you're
using JavaScript,

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.300
all the neat web
stuff that we've

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.100
done that work well
in JavaScript and well

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.722
in Node also work on
these robots, which

00:11:35.722 --> 00:11:38.180
is really cool, because you
can fall back on all the tricks

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.596
and techniques and
design patterns

00:11:39.596 --> 00:11:42.510
that you learned from building
JavaScript web applications,

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:44.870
except you can use them on
electronics without having

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:46.828
to learn a whole new
language and a whole bunch

00:11:46.828 --> 00:11:47.934
of new frameworks.

00:11:47.934 --> 00:11:50.350
MARK MANDEL: So what does that
sort of development process

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:50.900
look like?

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:53.890
So if I'm writing
JavaScript and I have, I

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:58.109
guess, some sort physical
thing, how do I connect to it?

00:11:58.109 --> 00:11:59.150
What does that look like?

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.350
What sort of thing would I have?

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:01.960
I don't know.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:03.919
I don't know anything
about this stuff, really.

00:12:03.919 --> 00:12:05.335
JEN TONG: So
usually, what I steer

00:12:05.335 --> 00:12:07.610
people to if they just want
to get started is first,

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.250
use whatever you
have available if you

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:10.310
have something available.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:12.520
And what a lot of
people, turns out,

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:15.207
already have is a Raspberry
Pi and an Arduino Uno

00:12:15.207 --> 00:12:16.790
that they picked up
from a Kickstarter

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.670
or they got at a conference.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.910
And they're on some shelf
somewhere gathering dust.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:22.450
So that's usually
the first recipe

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:26.050
I give people, is they install
NodeJS on a Raspberry Pi

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:29.560
and then use a USB cable to
plug it into their Arduino Uno

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:31.500
and use a protocol
called Firmata-- which

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.140
comes with Arduino SDK-- to
communicate with the Arduino,

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.380
and then do all their
electronics work on the Arduino

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.440
because it is a wonderfully
robust and durable board

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.830
and is very tolerant of
mistakes that would burn out

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:44.170
other dev boards.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:45.484
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's good.

00:12:45.484 --> 00:12:47.150
That's one of the
things that I actually

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:49.380
learned quite quick,
was that, depending

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:53.300
on how you plug stuff, you
might burn it down, which yeah.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:56.780
It's kind of sad if you
only have one of them.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:58.900
Actually for me, one of
the biggest problems--

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.630
it was not the programming
part of it when I got started.

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:04.010
It was, for
instance, if you want

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:09.480
to have a little
LED that blinks,

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.550
you actually have to add
some resistors in the middle.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:16.400
And I didn't know
how to do that.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:17.980
What is your advice?

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:19.840
How do I learn these things?

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.790
JEN TONG: So my advice is not
to fear things going wrong,

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.239
and to copy things, where
possible, to get started,

00:13:25.239 --> 00:13:26.280
because it's always easy.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:27.360
Just like a software
project, it's

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:28.818
always easy to
start from something

00:13:28.818 --> 00:13:29.960
that's already working.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.990
So coincidentally, my
favorite JavaScript library--

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:35.940
Johnny-five, which is used
by a lot of NodeBots--

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:37.450
has a wonderful
documentation that

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:41.250
also is a really good
primer to electronics.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:44.450
So whether you get it from the
Johnny-five docs or the Arduino

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:48.020
Uno starter pack and the
instruction manual there,

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:51.670
all those things have pictures
of the circuit you should build

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:54.600
and then an explanation of
why they should work that way,

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:57.392
and usually code that goes
along with it to get it working.

00:13:57.392 --> 00:13:58.850
So at the very
beginning I wouldn't

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.225
spend a lot of
time trying to get

00:14:00.225 --> 00:14:01.830
a deep understanding of why.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:05.660
Just find pictures, copy
them, get them working,

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.900
and then explore and expect
things to burn up every once

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:12.630
in a while and get used to it.

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.920
And then your understanding
will grow over time,

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.570
and your bag of
tricks will improve,

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:20.355
and things will catch
on fire less often.

00:14:20.355 --> 00:14:21.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Hopefully.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:22.670
JEN TONG: Yes.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:24.730
MARK MANDEL: Now, do you
have a favorite getting

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:25.570
started project?

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:27.380
I hear blinking
light tends to be

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:29.040
the hello, world of
Internet of Things.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.607
Is that kind of the truth?

00:14:30.607 --> 00:14:31.190
JEN TONG: Yep.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:33.760
So along with that recipe I
described with Raspberry Pi,

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.670
with Johnny-five and
Firmata and NodeJS

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:39.430
and the Arduino Uno, the
first project I usually

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:43.040
recommend people is an internet
button that turns on an LED.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.800
So you wire up a button, and
when you press the button,

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.460
you send a signal
out to the Cloud.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.240
I usually steer people
towards Firebase

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:50.520
for their first project.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.870
You flip something in
Firebase, and then you

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.970
have a Firebase
client on the same

00:14:55.970 --> 00:15:00.720
or another Raspberry Pi
that receives that signal

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:02.762
and then turns on an LED
when the button is down.

00:15:02.762 --> 00:15:04.636
MARK MANDEL: Do you want
to talk a little bit

00:15:04.636 --> 00:15:07.080
about what Firebase is and
maybe how it fits in with IoT?

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.705
We haven't really
talked about Firebase

00:15:08.705 --> 00:15:10.110
too much on this podcast.

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:10.940
JEN TONG: Got it.

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:13.385
So Firebase was an
independent company

00:15:13.385 --> 00:15:16.450
that got acquired by
Google in late 2014.

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:20.430
And it is a way to-- it's
kind of like, generally,

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:22.930
a bag of goodies that you can
use to build apps, like web

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:24.510
apps and mobile apps.

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:27.510
And it works really great
with JavaScript and NodeJS,

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.460
which means it also
plays exceptionally well

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:30.960
with NodeBots.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.560
And the data model does
behind Firebase-- one

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:36.670
of the components of Firebase
being a realtime database--

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:40.212
is you have a big ball of
JSON in the sky, in the Cloud.

00:15:40.212 --> 00:15:44.590
And all of your apps and devices
can write to any location.

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:46.792
And they can also
listen to a location.

00:15:46.792 --> 00:15:48.250
And whenever one
of those locations

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:51.232
changes or has a new child
added or something gets moved,

00:15:51.232 --> 00:15:52.815
the Firebase client
will automatically

00:15:52.815 --> 00:15:56.400
call a callback function that
you specify in your code.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.880
And what this does is it
makes all the multi-threading

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.610
and synchronization stuff-- it
kind of handles that for you.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.630
So it makes building
Internet of Things devices

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:06.090
so much easier than
having polling loops

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.290
and stuff like that.

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:09.415
MARK MANDEL: So it sounds
like Firebase is probably

00:16:09.415 --> 00:16:11.662
one of your very much
recommended starting places of,

00:16:11.662 --> 00:16:13.620
all right, I've got all
these devices out there

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.703
and I need to synchronize
data between them either

00:16:15.703 --> 00:16:16.780
coming in or going out.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:18.320
I can just plug it
all into Firebase

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.955
and then, hopefully, magic
happens and everything's OK.

00:16:20.955 --> 00:16:21.580
JEN TONG: Yeah.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:25.330
Firebase excels at prototyping
for web and mobile apps.

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.440
And for the same strengths--
the same reasons--

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:30.490
it also excels at
prototyping IoT devices.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.410
So it is wonderfully
awesome, especially

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:34.350
if you're building
one of something,

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.734
which is usually the first
step on an IoT project.

00:16:36.734 --> 00:16:37.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:41.220
So dwell a little
bit on Firebase.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.050
We used it for Cloud Spin.

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:45.640
And one of the good
things about it

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:48.570
is that you build whatever
you want to build on Firebase,

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:50.584
and then at some point
you're like, oh wait.

00:16:50.584 --> 00:16:52.000
We need a visualizer
to understand

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:53.429
that the system is doing.

00:16:53.429 --> 00:16:55.970
And that's super simple to do
because all the data is already

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:56.539
on the Cloud.

00:16:56.539 --> 00:16:58.330
So you don't need to
export anything extra.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:01.470
You can just build a
new, really tiny web

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.990
page that shows whatever
you're interested on.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:07.390
So yeah, if people have
not tried [INAUDIBLE],

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:09.366
I think it's a great
place to start.

00:17:09.366 --> 00:17:09.990
JEN TONG: Yeah.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:11.960
And up to 100
concurrent users is

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.849
part of our
developer plan, which

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:15.940
is free-- free forever,
which is awesome.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:16.839
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:17.579
That's very cool.

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:19.510
Very, very cool.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:21.829
So I have to ask.

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:24.540
What have been your
favorite IoT projects?

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:26.970
Maybe something weird and
wacky, something somebody's

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:29.667
done that seems a bit
interesting and cool?

00:17:29.667 --> 00:17:31.750
JEN TONG: So a lot of
people are interested in IoT

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.680
for saving money,
establishing new businesses,

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:36.090
and that kind of stuff.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.756
But my favorite
projects tend to go back

00:17:37.756 --> 00:17:40.056
to the crazy LED-powered
artsy scene that's

00:17:40.056 --> 00:17:41.680
really, really popular,
especially here

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:43.190
around San Francisco.

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:47.020
And because of the declining
cost of light emitting diodes,

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:49.800
blinky lights, and sensors
and all that stuff,

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:53.400
people are building these
really cool giant installation

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:56.770
or even small desktop sets
of interactive artwork

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.110
that are internet connected
and allow you to interact

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:00.610
with the artwork
and multiple people

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:02.250
around you in really cool ways.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:03.000
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.050
So like setting up sensors where
people walk through or touch

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:08.029
or play, and it sort
of responds in some

00:18:08.029 --> 00:18:09.320
interesting and wonderful ways.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:09.620
JEN TONG: Yeah.

00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:11.240
That popular thing I'm
starting to see a lot

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:13.406
is all of us are walking
around with little internet

00:18:13.406 --> 00:18:16.376
computers-- our smartphones--
and that's a really, really

00:18:16.376 --> 00:18:17.750
great way to have
people interact

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:21.120
with some other already
internet-connected device.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:22.150
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:23.520
Very, very cool.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:26.064
So you also
mentioned-- I just want

00:18:26.064 --> 00:18:27.980
to make sure it's very
clear for our listeners

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:29.646
as well-- you've
mentioned a whole bunch

00:18:29.646 --> 00:18:32.470
of different hardware,
so like Arduino

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.060
and a couple of other things.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:35.820
Is that your favorite
hardware to use?

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.620
Or do you have other favorite
things that you like to use?

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:40.578
JEN TONG: So usually what
I recommend to people

00:18:40.578 --> 00:18:42.194
is to use what you already have.

00:18:42.194 --> 00:18:44.402
Anything that's-- so if you
have anything that's like

00:18:44.402 --> 00:18:47.460
a Raspberry Pi, which is like a
board that runs a full computer

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.074
that runs an operating system,
you can probably run Node

00:18:50.074 --> 00:18:51.740
and Johnny-five on
it, which is awesome.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:53.810
You can go to the web page and
there's a huge list of boards.

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:56.540
So if you have like an Edison
or one of the other boards,

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:57.772
it will still work on there.

00:18:57.772 --> 00:19:00.230
So I usually encourage people
to use what you already have.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:02.960
But the reason, given
no other assumptions,

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:05.665
I steer people towards
the Pi and the Uno

00:19:05.665 --> 00:19:08.349
as a pair is because
they're ubiquitous.

00:19:08.349 --> 00:19:09.140
They're everywhere.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:10.565
People have lots of them.

00:19:10.565 --> 00:19:12.690
The documentation and
community around both of them

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:14.810
is very, very rich and active.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.190
And in case you do accidentally
burn one of them up,

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:18.834
they're pretty low cost.

00:19:18.834 --> 00:19:20.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That sounds good.

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.583
MARK MANDEL: Actually, talk
a little bit about low cost.

00:19:25.481 --> 00:19:27.730
Has there been a certain
shift over the certain number

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.790
of years and that's what's
made this so popular,

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:32.480
because of the shift
of cost coming down?

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:34.800
Has that been, I'm guessing,
a fairly large factor

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:36.325
in sort of the IoT space?

00:19:36.325 --> 00:19:36.950
JEN TONG: Yeah.

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:38.630
If you look back
even a few years ago,

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:42.850
it was much more expensive to
get a device onto the internet,

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:44.972
mostly because of the
complexity in the software.

00:19:44.972 --> 00:19:47.180
For example, when you're
writing code on an Arduino--

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:49.060
if you're just coding
on the Arduino--

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:51.500
you don't have an
operating system, usually.

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.370
So you lack things like virtual
memory and, importantly,

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.800
an internet stack and
an interim controller.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:59.850
And then the rise of these
tiny, tiny, tiny full-fledged

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.020
computers suddenly
made it much easier

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:04.160
to get things connected
to the internet.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:06.460
And around the same
time, the hardware

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:08.890
that allows you to connect
to Wi-Fi, like our existing

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:11.702
internet infrastructure, also
came down at the same time.

00:20:11.702 --> 00:20:13.660
So it was kind of a
convergence of those things

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:15.410
over the last few years
that have suddenly

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:19.227
made this so much easier and
so much more fun to hack on.

00:20:19.227 --> 00:20:21.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Could
you give a little bit

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:23.886
of what you think might
happen in the future?

00:20:23.886 --> 00:20:25.760
Do you think that
it's going to keep

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.140
on going and going to have
more and more little computers

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:29.056
everywhere?

00:20:29.056 --> 00:20:30.430
What is your vision
of the future

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:30.760
of the Internet of Things?

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:32.240
JEN TONG: What is my vision?

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:33.524
That's a big question.

00:20:33.524 --> 00:20:33.921
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is.

00:20:33.921 --> 00:20:35.470
MARK MANDEL: And if
you get it wrong,

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:36.710
then we're going
to come after you.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:37.130
And--

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:37.410
JEN TONG: Oh, no!

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:39.535
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it
is recorded forever, so--

00:20:39.535 --> 00:20:40.820
JEN TONG: That's so scary.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:45.200
So my vision is we're just
going to get better and better

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:46.930
at building these electronics.

00:20:46.930 --> 00:20:48.638
We're also going to
get better and better

00:20:48.638 --> 00:20:50.800
at coming up with
the bag of tricks

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.650
for making these things work.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.450
So on the electronics side, I
expect things to get cheaper.

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:57.510
Like one board that
came out recently

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:58.680
came almost out of nowhere.

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:02.760
It's called the ESP 8266, which
is this tiny little Wi-Fi chip

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.480
that's like $2.00.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.020
And I'm starting to tinker
around a lot with it now.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.560
And it's like the
cheapest way I've

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.830
seen to get devices onto Wi-Fi.

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:13.365
And I know a lot of people are
researching these Bluetooth

00:21:13.365 --> 00:21:15.740
mesh networks and stuff, but
those protocols aren't ready

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:16.240
yet.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:18.670
So everything I work on
now I usually use Wi-Fi

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:19.870
because it's already there.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:23.890
And seeing suddenly
a huge drop in cost

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:26.020
shows me that that's not
going to be a big blocker

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:29.280
and we're going to have lots
of low cost electronics.

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:30.780
And then on the
flip side of that

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.290
is kind of our software
and design patterns,

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:35.930
is we're still leaning a
lot on the bag of tricks

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:38.860
that we developed for solving
web problems, you know,

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:42.050
loading web pages and
all that kind of stuff.

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:45.881
But as the way we build web
apps changes and mobile apps,

00:21:45.881 --> 00:21:48.380
we're starting to come up with
new techniques for the way we

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:51.137
use caches and the way we
handle bursts of messages

00:21:51.137 --> 00:21:53.470
and that kind of stuff, which
is going to make it easier

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:56.560
and easier to use existing
services, APIs, and open source

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.594
projects to solve
your IoT problems.

00:21:59.594 --> 00:22:00.260
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:01.980
Very nice.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:04.330
All right, well thank you
very much for joining us, Jen.

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.759
Is there anything at all that
we haven't talked about that you

00:22:07.759 --> 00:22:08.550
want to talk about?

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:10.800
Have we missed anything?

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:13.670
JEN TONG: I guess though the one
thing that I would definitely

00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:15.920
put out there is sometimes
people kind of go down

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:18.730
a rabbit hole on how they would
build a million of something

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:20.740
or a thousand of
something before they even

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:24.540
get their first jumper cable
into their bread board.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:26.250
I would encourage
people to not focus

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:28.670
too much on the manufacturing
side, at least not

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:30.040
at the very beginning.

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:30.800
Spend time.

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:33.008
Like if you have an idea
you want to build something,

00:22:33.008 --> 00:22:34.090
build one of something.

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:35.890
Don't worry about the
manufacturing later.

00:22:35.890 --> 00:22:37.550
And then use that
one prototype you've

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:39.730
built to validate
your idea and get user

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.725
feedback, much the same way we
build mockups and scaffolding

00:22:42.725 --> 00:22:44.640
in web applications.

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:45.660
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:48.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Well, thank
you so much for all the insight.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:49.870
That was a very
interesting conversation.

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:50.690
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:51.450
JEN TONG: Yep.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:52.080
You're very welcome.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:52.900
It was a lot of fun.

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:53.899
Thanks for having me on.

